#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100688 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a46e688b-117f-5394-bcbb-fbfb7a5b6c7b" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Logistic Growth Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "a16c2c4f-38c3-5e8c-8962-3ad6c956748a" :question/math-academy-id "e-7321" :question/problem "The growth rate of the hamster population $P(t)$ in a park is jointly proportional to the hamster population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the park's carrying capacity and the hamster population. If the constant of proportionality is $0.005$ and the maximum possible hamster population is $120,$ which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the hamster population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the hamster population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the hamster population, the hamster population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.005,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=120.$ Therefore, the hamster population is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 0.005P(120-P).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100690 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a4751337-93bf-5b47-8b3f-32100820b19a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Logistic Growth Differential Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "29ee4686-7e38-5000-9d89-58e572f84cd7" :question/math-academy-id "e-7404" :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations is a logistic growth equation?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 56y-7y^2$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 56-7y^2$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 56y^2-7$" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFrom the given options, the only logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 56y-7y^2.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 7y(8-y).\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality $k=7,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319730 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a4751337-93bf-5b47-8b3f-32100820b19a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Logistic Growth Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be6f7be1-7440-5e9a-9219-a14af447508f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=4y(50-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319732} {:answer/id #uuid "ee2e3df5-dc10-57e7-b50b-63b69c25197b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=4y(50+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319733} {:answer/id #uuid "2d826549-ca11-54ea-85d6-cf3d6296233e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=4y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319734} {:answer/id #uuid "96165f7a-8a7a-50c2-9f47-3acbe5dfe02b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=4(50-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319735} {:answer/id #uuid "a9d9fad4-4275-565e-8ab6-b15dbd65e1ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=50-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be6f7be1-7440-5e9a-9219-a14af447508f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=4y(50-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319732} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f26d38e-4db9-5ec4-b951-cc06db6455cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319731}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1fd05112-8082-5953-be66-8ced5469a502" :question/math-academy-id "q-119264" :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations is a logistic growth equation?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFrom the given options, the only logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=4y(50-y).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=4,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=50.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319737 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a4751337-93bf-5b47-8b3f-32100820b19a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Logistic Growth Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85ba5ff4-abca-54d0-a88e-95ae30423a96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=27y+9{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319739} {:answer/id #uuid "f0f8d393-d1a2-527f-a108-fda56d53ea63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-9{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319740} {:answer/id #uuid "421bce6b-0f5f-57ce-89af-a5b4684e9b7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=27y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319741} {:answer/id #uuid "fad1b1f3-bfed-59b4-839f-9c3042a72ba2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=27-9{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319742} {:answer/id #uuid "2581acc5-d8e7-585b-9a5d-ec1f5f66e92a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=27y-9{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2581acc5-d8e7-585b-9a5d-ec1f5f66e92a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=27y-9{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319743} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2e9f263-e02d-59ac-aa30-3da14e631dce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "807017aa-19fe-525f-a1ee-8b8d7df357f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-119269" :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations is a logistic growth equation?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFrom the given options, the only logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=27y-9{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=9y(3-y).\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality is $k=9,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319744 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a4751337-93bf-5b47-8b3f-32100820b19a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Logistic Growth Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e41bf57f-17c5-51cc-b071-51bc9f559399" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=0.5{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319746} {:answer/id #uuid "4edfdba6-0cdf-5884-883d-2a510969693f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=5y+0.5{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319747} {:answer/id #uuid "1470a92c-5b82-52d1-a878-034a18384c22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=5y-0.5{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319748} {:answer/id #uuid "f574e06e-877b-5ece-9e51-f3d45d25db03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=5y-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319749} {:answer/id #uuid "f5e32d2a-ad0c-5eda-8480-dacb2f523f0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=5-0.5{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1470a92c-5b82-52d1-a878-034a18384c22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=5y-0.5{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319748} :answer-field/id #uuid "b87f9a81-f4de-5ac2-b119-280b960d0424" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "34eec7b3-07f3-5e03-82f6-3aa612f18378" :question/math-academy-id "q-119270" :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations is a logistic growth equation?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFrom the given options, the only logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=5y-0.5{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=0.5y(10-y).\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality is $k=0.5,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319751 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a46e688b-117f-5394-bcbb-fbfb7a5b6c7b" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Logistic Growth Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6073291-863b-57c6-8965-16e7deee3c89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=-0.000\\,8P(200\\,000-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319753} {:answer/id #uuid "6826a2d7-b44b-52a4-9832-6e389ab2f41c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=200\\,000P(0.000\\,8+P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319754} {:answer/id #uuid "519dc32d-05f4-56f6-8e4b-53a2c3c2f0a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,8P(200\\,000-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319755} {:answer/id #uuid "17513126-9098-5f2c-9081-7d7f4e7f35df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=200\\,000P(0.000\\,8-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319756} {:answer/id #uuid "81823732-60f8-512c-ad75-61dec652dac4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,8P(200\\,000+P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "519dc32d-05f4-56f6-8e4b-53a2c3c2f0a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,8P(200\\,000-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319755} :answer-field/id #uuid "7778b631-9e5f-5522-b242-a60348017bf5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d40c8729-9db6-5390-809b-b4eab15a380e" :question/math-academy-id "q-119286" :question/problem "The growth rate of the population $P(t)$ in a certain city is jointly proportional to the population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the city's carrying capacity and the population. If the constant of proportionality is $0.000\\,8$ and the maximum possible population is $200\\,000,$ which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the population, the population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,8,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=200\\,000.$ Therefore, the population is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,8P(200\\,000-P).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319758 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a46e688b-117f-5394-bcbb-fbfb7a5b6c7b" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Logistic Growth Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb113e85-eb3c-5f6a-9feb-a1213b151d63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=300P(0.2-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319760} {:answer/id #uuid "6c31edb2-41b9-50e7-ab95-d625fc3a1ed7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=300P(0.2+P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319761} {:answer/id #uuid "ee2a660b-9b75-576e-a007-b9ef67f741ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.2P(300+P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319762} {:answer/id #uuid "01b6cc5a-3d67-5d31-ae64-c6c0a704c025" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.2P(300-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319763} {:answer/id #uuid "e05cda94-a581-57e7-b9e4-c2a52804a26e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=-0.2P(300-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01b6cc5a-3d67-5d31-ae64-c6c0a704c025" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.2P(300-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319763} :answer-field/id #uuid "453ecb49-37df-542f-8f0b-94f362228a8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319759}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "536cae2e-8257-53cb-8c63-c22406092855" :question/math-academy-id "q-119289" :question/problem "A pest infestation is spreading through a land with $300$ trees. The rate of growth in the number of trees $P(t)$ infected with the pest is jointly proportional to the number of trees that are infected at time $t,$ measured in months,  and the number of uninfected trees. If the constant of proportionality is $0.2,$ which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the growth rate of infected trees is *jointly* proportional to the number of trees that are infected at time $t,$ and the number of uninfected trees, the number of infected trees follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.2,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=300.$ Therefore, the number of trees that are infected is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.2P(300-P).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319765 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a46e688b-117f-5394-bcbb-fbfb7a5b6c7b" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Logistic Growth Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9be55eb9-b788-5812-919c-d035a3d4d199" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=50\\,000\\,000P(0.3-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319767} {:answer/id #uuid "63917650-9684-5a9c-9cc1-5571500b3034" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.3P(50\\,000\\,000+P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319768} {:answer/id #uuid "4e8fca54-4be0-5159-a1d2-6a0976e5bbfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=-0.3P(50\\,000\\,000-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319769} {:answer/id #uuid "5b5fce32-5f1c-5a33-ab12-b3d7ace6aeee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=50\\,000\\,000P(0.3+P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319770} {:answer/id #uuid "766379fd-f286-583e-84bd-2d89b2ea1e3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.3P(50\\,000\\,000-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319771}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "766379fd-f286-583e-84bd-2d89b2ea1e3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.3P(50\\,000\\,000-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319771} :answer-field/id #uuid "582cdc7e-177c-5c41-b968-798af11686b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319766}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ab19698-b92f-5298-98aa-22d4962cc90f" :question/math-academy-id "q-119290" :question/problem "A population of $50\\,000\\,000$ people is being vaccinated against a disease. The rate of growth in the number of people $P(t)$ vaccinated is jointly proportional to the number of people who have been vaccinated at time $t,$ measured in months, and the number of unvaccinated people. If the constant of proportionality is $0.3,$ which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the growth rate of vaccinated people is *jointly* proportional to the number of people who have been vaccinated at time $t,$ and the number of unvaccinated people, the number of vaccinated people follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.3,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=50\\,000\\,000.$ Therefore, the number of people who have been vaccinated is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.3P(50\\,000\\,000-P).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319772 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a4751337-93bf-5b47-8b3f-32100820b19a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Logistic Growth Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cbbd473a-efc1-5dd7-89fd-362da4840b38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=2+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319774} {:answer/id #uuid "f47bb48e-f726-5081-bee3-f911e95dbbe0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=y(2+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319775} {:answer/id #uuid "864e9e8e-82ee-5eb3-bcc0-4897a85f466e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=y(2-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319776} {:answer/id #uuid "a6dac05f-5e1a-5b71-b46f-946fd5380aaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=2-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319777} {:answer/id #uuid "d52a6b29-feed-5ec0-ac22-5efd37bf17bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319778}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "864e9e8e-82ee-5eb3-bcc0-4897a85f466e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=y(2-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319776} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec64378b-4147-5fff-833b-fc7371f5e4d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bd6adb4d-cd72-54d4-abec-2e57a7ce7b95" :question/math-academy-id "q-83848" :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations is a logistic growth equation?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFrom the given options, the only logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=y(2-y).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=1,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319779 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a4751337-93bf-5b47-8b3f-32100820b19a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Logistic Growth Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer